  "articleSummary": "The article, \"No Fate But What We Bill,\" argues that the agentic AI threat is not one dramatic breakthrough but two ordinary, compounding curves: how long a single agent can work autonomously (endurance, citing METR's time-horizon doubling data) and how many sub-agents one orchestrator can coordinate (breadth, citing Anthropic's disclosure of the GTG-1002 espionage campaign run through Claude Code). Its central claim is that attackers multiply these into a serious threat, and that stolen access, not purchased capability, is the real danger, as in \"LLMjacking,\" where victims pay the token bill; the article also uses probability math (1 − (1 − p)^n) to show that cheap, repeated attempts make per-attempt defenses insufficient. The tone is a sharp, opinionated analysis with a rhetorical, slightly rant-like edge, framed around a recurring Terminator metaphor (the \"absolutely will not stop\" line, the robot-versus-Skynet distinction, \"the swarm only has to be right once\"). It is aimed at technical decision-makers and security/engineering teams who dismiss agentic risk as hype, and it uses concise, conceptual figures and formulas to make its case.",
